with its own data.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.760
And of course, all of these
have their own platforms

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.610
and systems and tools
for us to kind of wrestle

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:02.700
with to bring this together.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:06.270
And coordinating all of that
is, I think, Pretty, much

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.350
a universal challenge.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:09.540
I mean obviously the
automotive industry

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.070
has its eccentricities.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.320
But of course I think
every marketer really

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.600
sees this landscape and
they say, how can we create

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.290
a single view of the customer?

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:24.050
And to talk a little bit more
about the actual scale here--

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:30.580
so while I think that Mercedes--

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.640
it should be easy
to make this choice.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.260
It's a superior product and
it should be an easy decision.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:42.549
But the car buying experience,
it's a long consideration

00:12:42.549 --> 00:12:43.090
cycle, right?

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:44.140
It's a big decision.

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:45.880
There's a lot of
planning and research

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:47.430
that goes into
making that decision.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:51.490
And so over these sort of
months long decision process,

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.320
we have so many interactions
and touch points.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.430
So think about the millions of
customers, all the platforms we

00:12:57.430 --> 00:12:59.590
were just talking
about, and look

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:02.140
at that over three to six
months while someone is really

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:03.130
making this decision.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:05.740
We're talking about
billions upon billions

00:13:05.740 --> 00:13:08.260
of individual interactions.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.240
And we can't really
make sense of those

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.490
without using
statistical models.

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:14.320
And so one of the
things that we do

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.430
is we have to use models
to kind of systematically

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.471
help us determine who the right
people are to target, what

00:13:21.471 --> 00:13:22.720
decisions we should be making.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.430
And so that comes
with its own challenge

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:28.150
because then it takes time and
resources to develop, build,

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:29.590
test the models.

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:32.410
And that process doesn't
always lend itself

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:38.590
to your more real time use cases
like serving digital media.

00:13:38.590 --> 00:13:45.960
And on top of this, so there's
this initial Google Cloud

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.810
for Marketers rollout
that happened last fall.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:52.050
And they talked about
some of the benefits

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:54.780
and some of what they
envision for this platform.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:57.950
And it talked about collecting,
analyzing, visualizing,

00:13:57.950 --> 00:13:59.040
and iterating on data.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.860
And I think that's a
very, very good way

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.200
to position the value
of this for marketers.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.890
But for the future
marketing collateral

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.060
for Cloud for Marketers,
we're adding one more.

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:12.960
We're going to add activate,
because we also see

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.180
this as an activation platform.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:18.450
And if you're going to create
an ecosystem where you integrate

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:20.332
all of your customer
data across the board,

00:14:20.332 --> 00:14:22.290
I think there are a lot
of additional use cases

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.080
that you can activate outside
of just the sort of time

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:28.860
to insight and driving
efficiencies in the analytics

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.570
process.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:34.310
And so just sort of an
overview of our challenges

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.410
and how we're addressing
them with the cloud.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:39.260
As far as data collection goes,
we have all these platforms.

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.240
But actually a lot of
them, as Matt mentioned,

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:43.730
are already on Google platforms.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:46.400
And so we were really able to
speed up the data collection

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:48.980
process by taking
advantage of that.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.050
As far as analysis
goes, we were kind

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:54.290
of building an EDW within
BigQuery, which as we know

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:57.350
is very fast at produced
the querying time.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:15:01.710
And also, we don't have any
operational concerns anymore.

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.900
As far as visualization
goes, a lot of our use case

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:06.750
is actually--

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.810
we were using Google
Data Studio actually

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:12.210
as a dashboarding tool,
which actually it's

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:13.690
met most of our needs so far.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:16.080
And obviously we can integrate
with additional platforms

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.270
if we need to.

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.860
And as far as iteration
goes, all of that time

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:22.560
we were talking about that
goes into developing models,

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.910
we're able to automate
them now in the cloud.

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.120
And that's really
speeding up that process.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.280
And then as far as
activation goes,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.220
we also have all
of these use cases

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:36.046
around personalization
for the site and for CRM,

00:15:36.046 --> 00:15:37.920
and then also things
like audience management

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.200
for media, which we see as a
really, really big opportunity

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:40.700
here.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.040
So we really see a lot of
extensibility to this platform

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.627
and a lot of potential use
cases that we can apply it to.

00:15:46.627 --> 00:15:48.210
And with that I'm
going to hand it off

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.230
to Jeremy, who's going to talk
through our actual architecture

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.670
and our experience so far
working with Google Cloud.

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:55.170
Thanks.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:16:00.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:01.250
JEREMY TERUYA: Hello.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:01.750
I'm Jeremy.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:04.990
I'm associate director
here at SapientRazorfish.

00:16:04.990 --> 00:16:08.380
So I'm going to walk through
some of the actual architecture

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:11.840
of how this was accomplished.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.220
So this is essentially our
basic architecture right now.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:17.650
As Evan mentioned, there's a
lot of disparate data sources

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:18.316
that we collect.

00:16:18.316 --> 00:16:20.380
So these are just a few
examples right now--

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:22.600
DoubleClick, Google
Analytics 360, YouTube,

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.610
Kenshoo with paid
search, even some stuff

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:27.040
from MBIT for CRM
for after-sales,

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:30.645
dealership relationships.

00:16:30.645 --> 00:16:32.020
So kind of the
basic walkthrough.

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:33.942
And I should probably
put icons there labeling

00:16:33.942 --> 00:16:34.900
what each of these are.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:38.140
So if you're unfamiliar with
the actual Google products,

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.360
this may not be that beneficial.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:41.920
But from reading
left to right, it's

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:44.170
essentially we do use a
combination of Dataflow

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:45.990
and the connectors or
Data Transfer Service

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.720
that Matt Tai mentioned was
announced earlier today.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.840
We load, during the
daily ETL process,

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:56.980
Google Cloud Storage backups
in case anything goes awry.

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:58.360
That's going down.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:00.790
And then reading to the
right, BigQuery obviously

00:17:00.790 --> 00:17:04.119
is our enterprise data
warehouse solution.

00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:06.010
Visualization through
Google Data Studio

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:07.510
and a mix of Tableau,
just depending

00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:11.345
on what our individual
stakeholders need.

00:17:11.345 --> 00:17:13.720
And then moving more toward
the automation and activation

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:15.344
portion now, Evan
had mentioned that we

00:17:15.344 --> 00:17:18.700
use a lot of more modeling
purposes and statistical means

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:20.869
to make sense of all
of this information.

00:17:20.869 --> 00:17:22.869
So we've been playing
around with Data Lab

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:25.630
over the course over the
last three to six months.

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:27.460
But a lot of our
major development work

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.410
is done on Jupyter
as a platform.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.540
And we use Dataproc
as a way to automate

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:32.890
a lot of the modeling code.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:35.089
So that's on the far right.

00:17:35.089 --> 00:17:36.880
So a lot of the modeling
that's iterated on

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.430
is either reingested
back into BigQuery

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:44.920
as a way to validate and also
to monitor as a performance

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.980
mechanism, but also we
use specific and custom

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:49.660
integrations with
different platforms

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:50.750
to push information out.

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:53.170
So the activation portion
that Evan was talking about

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:57.170
is a really, really important
component to our success.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:17:59.680
So this entire solution
architecture right now

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:01.930
has officially been registered
as a partner solution

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:02.780
within Google.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.410
So this is essentially how
we've laid it out for Mercedes.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:09.725
And so kind of
the reasons why we

00:18:09.725 --> 00:18:11.100
went through this
whole migration

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.809
process-- so we
were on AWS before.

00:18:12.809 --> 00:18:14.850
I don't really need to
spend a whole lot of time.

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.281
I'm sure everyone in here has
seen all the demonstrations

00:18:17.281 --> 00:18:19.530
over the course of the last
day as well as potentially

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:20.115
in the past.

00:18:20.115 --> 00:18:22.320
BigQuery is very fast.

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:24.900
This is not as impressive
as the petabyte example

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:27.570
that they had shown at
the 12 o'clock session,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.940
but essentially what
we found is the speed

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:33.780
is really not determining how
many questions we ask anymore.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:38.280
I could go run a query across
gigabytes and gigabytes of data

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.710
or terabytes of data and
maybe go get a coffee

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:42.750
or go to a meeting
and when I come back,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:44.111
it may or may not be done.

00:18:44.111 --> 00:18:46.110
I don't really have to
worry about that anymore.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.600
I can kind of iterate
on my questions and all

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.570
of the questions from
Mercedes and get them back

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.234
in a timely manner.

00:18:53.234 --> 00:18:54.900
Also mitigating the
loss of productivity

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.020
due to human error--

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:58.200
so a lot of times
as our analysts

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.980
are running queries
against other solutions--

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:03.226
you write your code.

00:19:03.226 --> 00:19:03.850
You execute it.

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:08.040
You wait a good amount of
time and it comes out wrong.

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:11.010
I can mitigate that loss
because it's very, very fast

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.530
and I don't have to worry
about, oh crap, I just

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:16.710
messed up and wasted two hours.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:19.080
So this is essentially a basis.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.670
So we know BigQuery
was fast and we

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.380
know it was a
great architecture,

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.170
but we already had the
system set up in AWS.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.840
So really, was the
juice worth the squeeze

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:31.020
in terms of doing this
whole migration process?

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:34.500
One of the big hangups
for us was we've

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:38.040
architected a lot of custom
integrations into Redshift.

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.240
Do we want to repeat that again?

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:43.110
So with the connectors or Data
Transfer Service right now,

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:46.300
as Matt mentioned,
Mercedes is a brand

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:47.997
that has a lot of
products on Google.

00:19:47.997 --> 00:19:50.580
So leveraging the connectors was
a very, very simple solution.

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:52.860
So this is kind of like
a one week process for--

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:54.651
I believe this was
DoubleClick at the time.

00:19:54.651 --> 00:19:56.550
So for engineers,
this may or may not

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:59.520
be very similar to the processes
that you guys have architected

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:01.620
in the past of getting
that data transfer files

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.500
into a suitable SQL warehouse.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.790
But essentially
the gist of it is,

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:09.775
we've gone from
debugging Spark code

00:20:09.775 --> 00:20:12.150
and making sure that the
scripts are automating correctly

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:15.090
on an ETL process on
a daily fashion to,

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:18.062
can you or can you
not fill out a form?

00:20:18.062 --> 00:20:20.520
It's very, very simple to set
up the data transfer process.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.170
You literally open it up.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:23.670
You type in your network IDs.

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:25.710
You set a cadence,
and then it just goes.

00:20:25.710 --> 00:20:26.680
And it's very fast.

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:34.665
So we have enterprise
data warehouse.

00:20:34.665 --> 00:20:35.540
It's very, very fast.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:38.290
BigQuery, the connectors sped
up the infrastructure setup

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:39.590
as well.

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:42.310
So essentially we
reduce our delivery time

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.550
for performance reporting or
analysis from any questions

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:48.250
that Mercedes had by around 60%.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.890
So we clear the way for getting
a lot of the base questions

00:20:50.890 --> 00:20:53.740
down that they have, and as well
as increasing sophistication

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:56.198
of the questions that they
would like to ask because now we

00:20:56.198 --> 00:20:57.430
have a lot more time.

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:00.190
And this is like a little quote
from the last analytics trend

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.470
report from Deloitte last year.

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:05.099
"So big data and traditional
analytics are emerging.

00:21:05.099 --> 00:21:06.640
So it's really,
really getting harder

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:08.431
to distinguish the two
because the asks are

00:21:08.431 --> 00:21:10.010
getting more sophisticated."

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:12.310
So setting up a
platform like this

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:14.650
has allowed us to
scale our ability

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:18.610
to answer those questions in
a very, very timely manner.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:20.470
Increasing the time for
exploratory analysis

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:24.700
for Mercedes, as well as
has added benefit for us

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:29.020
at SapientRazorfish is,
essentially, you now

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:30.580
have this great architecture.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.040
Potentially there are other
great analytic storytellers

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.790
or great data analysts or
traditional business analysts

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:38.560
who may want to get
their hands on this

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:41.650
and sort of do a cross-train
against your department.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.260
For us at least it's been
a very, very great benefit.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:46.840
A lot of people have sort
of flocked over to us

00:21:46.840 --> 00:21:48.850
and sort of, we do
little training sessions

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:49.475
here and there.

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:55.610
So net-net, cost
times scale all have

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:58.320
improved over the
course of our migration.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:02.080
80% reduction in startup
costs, not just time and money.

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:04.550
BigQuery's very fast,
answer more questions

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:08.690
that you need answered,
increase your allocation

00:22:08.690 --> 00:22:10.470
to generate insight
as well as scale.

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:12.650
So scale was the
biggest thing for us.

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:15.330
As the questions become
more and more complex,

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:16.640
we need bigger horsepower.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:20.000
So while sometimes we just
use a general four core

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:21.890
whatever BigQuery
has, we sometimes

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:27.878
scale it up in
Dataproc as needed.

00:22:27.878 --> 00:22:30.130
And with that, I'll
hand it back to Matt.

