lower cost compared

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:45.732
to what it would be to have
all of that stuff in Redshift.

00:36:45.732 --> 00:36:47.730
DAMIAN LAWLOR: In terms
of the move to cloud,

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:52.130
is starting with the data and
analytics platform on cloud,

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:53.880
is that a good place
to start for somebody

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:56.733
who is maybe thinking
about the journey to cloud?

00:37:01.252 --> 00:37:01.960
PETER JAFFE: Yes.

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:05.830
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:05.830 --> 00:37:08.970
I mean, it's been some
years since I did analytics

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:12.340
on data sets that
were on-premise.

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:18.370
And it's just very
liberating, as the person

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:21.430
who's interacting with the
data, to have it on the cloud,

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:25.150
because it's so easy to spin
up additional resources,

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:29.980
to move data around, to
get data into the database

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:32.020
where you're going to
perform your analysis.

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:37.210
It's so easy to put Tableau
or Looker on top of that.

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:41.830
I can't imagine, at this
point, doing it any other way.

00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:43.330
ALLAN BEAUFOUR:
Data is really where

00:37:43.330 --> 00:37:45.400
you need elasticity and growth.

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:48.840
We're not going to get less
data in the world, right?

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:50.290
It's only going to come more.

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:51.430
It's going to be more
and more prevalent.

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:53.620
And whatever business
function you're running,

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:54.990
there will be more data.

00:37:54.990 --> 00:37:57.630
And doing that on-premise,
you'll be provisioning for now

00:37:57.630 --> 00:37:59.590
and forever.

00:37:59.590 --> 00:38:01.840
So yeah, I would say it's
a good place to start.

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.520
You need it.

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:04.950
DAMIAN LAWLOR:
And I remember you

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:07.320
telling a story in New
York about how actually it

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:09.882
was the data
analytics that spurred

00:38:09.882 --> 00:38:11.340
some thoughts about
other workloads

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:12.810
then moving over to the cloud.

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:15.590
Can you tell us a bit
more about that journey?

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:16.666
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: Yeah.

00:38:16.666 --> 00:38:20.870
So before BigQuery,
or rather I would say

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:24.330
BigQuery has really transformed
how a lot of analysts

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:27.690
do their work, because
they do the same work

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:30.630
but BigQuery has enabled
them to kind of stay

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:34.890
in this inquisitive, asking
the data questions mindset.

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:38.370
Whereas before, they kind
of came up with a theory,

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:41.760
did a query in either
Redshift or Hive,

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.520
and then went to lunch,
or went to a meeting,

00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:48.120
and then came back and oh, what
if I ask this question instead

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:49.540
or this and this and this?

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:51.480
Right now it's a much
more interactive session

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:54.690
with BigQuery, which
has really unleashed

00:38:54.690 --> 00:38:57.990
a lot of analytics and
thinking and creativity,

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.330
where they can query the
data, see something, come

00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:03.150
back, create another
way, add more filters.

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:06.822
It's a much more fluid
workload instead of context

00:39:06.822 --> 00:39:07.780
switching all the time.

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:09.630
You do this, go to
lunch, come back.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:10.860
Oh, what was I doing?

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:15.690
So it's really opened up
so many more things for us

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:18.642
to have a much more interactive
session with the data.

00:39:18.642 --> 00:39:20.850
DAMIAN LAWLOR: Just a quick
question to the audience.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:24.960
How many in the audience are
already doing data analytics

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:25.894
in the cloud?

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:31.760
OK, so then I think this
next question is relevant.

00:39:31.760 --> 00:39:35.090
So for those that aren't,
what are the pitfalls

00:39:35.090 --> 00:39:37.370
that you think people
should think through

00:39:37.370 --> 00:39:39.320
as they start this journey?

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:41.114
Peter, if you want to--

00:39:44.110 --> 00:39:47.020
PETER JAFFE: Pitfalls to moving
your data into the cloud we're

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:50.030
talking about-- yeah.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:55.270
Well, gee, that's a tough one.

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:57.390
I mean, there are
so many advantages

00:39:57.390 --> 00:39:59.190
to moving to the cloud.

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:04.290
I think that if you've got
a lot of infrastructure

00:40:04.290 --> 00:40:09.240
on top of your on-premise
data, you've got a lot of stuff

00:40:09.240 --> 00:40:18.990
to migrate, getting buy-in,
getting people on board,

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:20.970
figuring out what tools
you're going to use.

00:40:23.670 --> 00:40:28.650
My experience with data sets
that were all on-premise

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.170
was that the tools on top
of them were not that great.

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:37.850
It was a lot harder
to get insights out.

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:45.760
And maybe the specific tools
that are responsible for that.

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:51.840
But I don't think that aside
from getting your organization

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:55.650
behind it, that
there is a lot of--

00:40:55.650 --> 00:40:57.360
there's not a lot of danger.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:59.220
I think that people
are more afraid

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:01.595
that there's going
to be security gaps

00:41:01.595 --> 00:41:02.470
and things like that.

00:41:06.069 --> 00:41:08.110
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: I don't
know, like it's the same.

00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:09.990
I don't know if there
are any big pitfalls.

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:14.650
There are some challenges,
making sure your security

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:16.814
department, your
infosec department

00:41:16.814 --> 00:41:17.980
understands what it's about.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:20.920
And it's hard doing
data center security,

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:22.780
but Google actually
has a lot of people

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:26.050
that know how to do that, that
it's hard to compete with.

00:41:26.050 --> 00:41:28.310
It is easier to burn
through some money,

00:41:28.310 --> 00:41:29.459
if you're not careful.

00:41:29.459 --> 00:41:31.000
Like if you have
provisioned hardware

00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:34.280
you paid for, you're not
burning any more money there.

00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:37.060
So you can do some missteps
and burned through the money,

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:41.550
but set up some quotas
and that stops there.

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:42.560
DAMIAN LAWLOR: OK.

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:45.730
So you didn't-- one of the
things I hear from other people

00:41:45.730 --> 00:41:48.370
who are looking at this is how
difficult it can be to break

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:51.190
down the data silos or
actually get individual areas

00:41:51.190 --> 00:41:53.800
of their business to
cough up the data,

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:54.950
if we can put it like that.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:58.210
Was that an issue for
either of you, in terms of--

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:00.880
PETER JAFFE: That has not
been an issue at Hearst.

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:03.640
I mean I would say the
opposite, that everybody

00:42:03.640 --> 00:42:06.280
wants to have their data
in a central location.

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:09.250
And maybe this is organization
specific at Hearst,

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:15.970
but we have a lot of use
across the organization

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:17.460
with our common data set.

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:19.540
And in fact this has been
great in Google Cloud

00:42:19.540 --> 00:42:24.190
in particular versus what
we are experiencing in AWS.

00:42:24.190 --> 00:42:28.840
It's just been so easy
to provision users

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:31.240
and give them access
to different data sets,

00:42:31.240 --> 00:42:33.580
and not just data
sets in BigQuery,

00:42:33.580 --> 00:42:37.990
but other resources within the
cloud like Compute and Storage

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:41.040
and whatever else
you might want.

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:43.780
We have had such great
adoption this year,

00:42:43.780 --> 00:42:46.660
it's amazing to me how
many different people

00:42:46.660 --> 00:42:49.660
from different businesses across
the organization at Hearst

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:52.780
are using our data that we've
been wanting them to use

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:55.630
for years, and they've
been wanting to use,

00:42:55.630 --> 00:42:59.054
but it's just been harder
for them to get at it.

00:42:59.054 --> 00:43:00.470
ALLAN BEAUFOUR:
Yeah, I think once

00:43:00.470 --> 00:43:03.279
people-- we haven't
had too much trouble.

00:43:03.279 --> 00:43:04.820
There are some old
flaky systems that

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:06.528
are a little hard to
get the data out of,

00:43:06.528 --> 00:43:10.070
but it's not because people
have been actively resisting.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:13.680
But we were talking about that
just before, like once people--

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:16.010
you shouldn't
underestimate the benefit

00:43:16.010 --> 00:43:19.760
of having that Google Login and
a web UI on top of BigQuery.

00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:23.060
Like it sounds silly that that's
an enabler, but it really is.

00:43:23.060 --> 00:43:25.470
And once people see they
can just click a link,

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:26.690
they're already logged in.

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:27.890
Here's a SQL interface.

00:43:27.890 --> 00:43:29.812
And they see the speed
and the ease of it,

00:43:29.812 --> 00:43:31.520
getting to the data,
people are more than

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:33.394
happy to get their data
in there because they

00:43:33.394 --> 00:43:34.880
can see what that enables.

00:43:37.280 --> 00:43:38.780
DAMIAN LAWLOR: In
terms of enabling,

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:41.480
we had an interesting
conversation earlier on today

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:44.240
in terms of this concept of
a democratization of data

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:46.670
science, so the concept that
these tools were actually

00:43:46.670 --> 00:43:51.470
go into allow access to
data to a much broader group

00:43:51.470 --> 00:43:54.560
within the organization, and
that would unleash creativity

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:56.450
within your organization.

00:43:56.450 --> 00:44:00.225
Is that something you're
seeing or you believe in?

00:44:00.225 --> 00:44:01.100
Where are we on that?

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:08.010
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: So, yes and no.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:09.919
As I think also
said earlier, like

00:44:09.919 --> 00:44:11.460
after the initial
excitement, there's

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:14.760
usually some frustration
that follows quickly after.

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.300
Just having access to the data
doesn't mean you understand it.

00:44:18.300 --> 00:44:20.924
So you can give all
the self-service access

00:44:20.924 --> 00:44:23.340
to the data you want and people
poke around in it and then

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:26.490
come up with reports that
don't make a lot of sense,

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:30.150
and then reconciliation
nightmare afterwards.

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:33.420
So I 100% believe
in that data should

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:36.720
be accessible to everyone,
regulation allowing,

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:38.370
because it does open up.

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:41.414
But that it just makes data
self-service and everyone

00:44:41.414 --> 00:44:43.080
across the company
will just dig into it

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:46.350
and create their own
reports or data science,

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:50.640
yeah, I don't really see
that working out in practice.

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:52.500
DAMIAN LAWLOR: Anything
different in Hearst?

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:53.940
PETER JAFFE: No,
I would generally

00:44:53.940 --> 00:44:57.330
agree with everything
Allan said.

00:44:57.330 --> 00:45:01.260
There are some
products coming out now

00:45:01.260 --> 00:45:03.360
that are trying to get at that.

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:09.900
And with the data transfer
from like DFP into BigQuery--

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:12.720
Looker's just been
experimenting with this thing

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:17.320
they call blocks which is like
these ready made dashboards

00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:19.860
that you can plug in
on top of your DFP

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:22.320
that has been imported
through the data transfer tool

00:45:22.320 --> 00:45:23.610
into your BigQuery.

00:45:23.610 --> 00:45:26.319
And a couple of
people at Hearst have

00:45:26.319 --> 00:45:28.860
been playing around with that
and it looks kind of promising.

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:32.670
So I think that maybe we'll
get in that direction more.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:38.370
But I generally completely
agree that opening it up

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:42.615
to people who were not
inclined to analyze the data

00:45:42.615 --> 00:45:44.490
is not going to get them
to analyze the data.

00:45:48.375 --> 00:45:50.000
DAMIAN LAWLOR: Some
of the case studies

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:52.340
we've heard from
earlier, they talked

00:45:52.340 --> 00:45:55.040
about how the benefits,
the actual business

00:45:55.040 --> 00:45:58.120
outcomes that they were
able to drive from this move

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:00.660
to cloud data and analytics.

00:46:00.660 --> 00:46:03.400
Are you comfortable to share
any of the transformations

00:46:03.400 --> 00:46:05.330
or any of the positive
impacts that you've

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:06.952
had in your own organizations?

00:46:09.628 --> 00:46:12.979
PETER JAFFE: I can talk about
specific products and things

00:46:12.979 --> 00:46:14.020
that we've built on that.

00:46:14.020 --> 00:46:21.190
I mean, we take our click
data and pull it off

00:46:21.190 --> 00:46:25.540
of our 180 odd websites and
put it through a pipeline

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:31.270
and grab the impressions
that come back from DFP.

00:46:31.270 --> 00:46:36.370
And I've got a real time
revenue estimation tool

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:38.440
that is built on top of that.

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:40.600
So I'm pulling in all
the clicks and I'm

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:44.990
pulling in the impressions
and I'm merging that together

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:47.620
and I'm estimating how much
revenue is being generated.

00:46:47.620 --> 00:46:51.780
I'm pushing that out
to a tool that, it's

00:46:51.780 --> 00:46:55.810
got a dashboard on top of it
that our traffic buyers use

00:46:55.810 --> 00:46:58.330
to see high performing CPMs.

00:46:58.330 --> 00:47:03.340
And they look for opportunities
to buy traffic at lower CPMs

00:47:03.340 --> 00:47:05.830
and push it to those
URLs that are currently

00:47:05.830 --> 00:47:06.880
earning high CPMs.

00:47:06.880 --> 00:47:10.510
So that's a nice
example, I think,

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:19.019
of real world revenue being
driven off of the data.

00:47:19.019 --> 00:47:20.060
DAMIAN LAWLOR: Thank you.

00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:20.561
Allan?

00:47:20.561 --> 00:47:22.851
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: We're a little
further behind than that

00:47:22.851 --> 00:47:23.820
in our--

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:25.930
it's more exploration
right now, more [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:25.930 --> 00:47:28.480
enabled, more data
sets in BigQuery

00:47:28.480 --> 00:47:30.910
that we can join
together has enabled more

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:32.890
exploratory analysis for us.

00:47:32.890 --> 00:47:35.100
For example, in advertising,
getting DFP data out

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:39.130
and our click traffic in there
and exploring other advertising

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:42.860
scenarios and how that would
actually affect our revenue.

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:45.430
But we're not where we
actually build product.

00:47:45.430 --> 00:47:48.100
We've migrated products,
data products into BigQuery,

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:49.690
but we haven't
built any new ones.

00:47:49.690 --> 00:47:51.829
So I have no fancy things yet.

00:47:51.829 --> 00:47:54.370
DAMIAN LAWLOR: Well I think that
leads into my next question,

00:47:54.370 --> 00:47:55.830
which is, what's next?

00:47:55.830 --> 00:47:57.510
So where do you see--

00:47:57.510 --> 00:47:59.820
what are you looking
forward to building out

00:47:59.820 --> 00:48:02.610
over the next 12 to 18 months?

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:04.850
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: Oh, a lot.

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:07.050
More data sets, obviously--
the more data we

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:09.107
can get in and make
it a join away,

00:48:09.107 --> 00:48:11.190
and then clean up all the
data quality issues that

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:14.820
will come out of seeing
the data together.

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:17.820
No, for me, there's a
couple of key things--

00:48:17.820 --> 00:48:19.650
building data
products, but getting

00:48:19.650 --> 00:48:23.640
the funnel of from a prototype
on a data scientist's

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:27.150
laptop to a data product
running in production,

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:28.930
getting that streamlined.

00:48:28.930 --> 00:48:31.810
So really empowering
our data science team

00:48:31.810 --> 00:48:36.390
to do all their crazy stuff with
R, Python notebooks, whatever,

00:48:36.390 --> 00:48:38.940
and then putting the
machinery and the platform

00:48:38.940 --> 00:48:41.199
together underneath
that makes it very easy

00:48:41.199 --> 00:48:42.990
for them to run that
in production and feed

00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:45.360
into, whether they put
stuff into BigQuery

00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:48.210
or feed to our product
or whatever it is.

00:48:48.210 --> 00:48:50.770
There's a lot of commonalities
between all data products.

00:48:50.770 --> 00:48:53.220
And getting that up so we
can really empower the data

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:56.100
scientists, it's really
something I really, really

00:48:56.100 --> 00:48:57.420
look forward to.

00:48:57.420 --> 00:49:00.900
And then part of that is closing
kind of the feedback loop.

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:03.300
A lot of what we've been
striving with Pub/Sub,

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:05.150
Dataflow, BigQuery--

00:49:05.150 --> 00:49:06.900
maybe not BigQuery in
that sense actually.

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:09.420
But it's streaming, have
most of our data streaming.

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:11.560
I want more streaming
data sets in there

00:49:11.560 --> 00:49:14.490
so we can do real time models
that feed into the product,

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:17.550
change the product behavior,
then see the user actions

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:20.530
flow back into that and close
that feedback loop in many more

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:21.030
ways.

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:23.190
That's what I'm really,
really excited to see.

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:24.180
DAMIAN LAWLOR: So going
back to the comment that

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:25.846
was there earlier
about activation being

00:49:25.846 --> 00:49:27.450
a really important
outcome of this,

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:31.470
that we want to move past just
analysis to actually automate

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:34.342
the outcomes and then use those
outcomes to drive the product.

00:49:34.342 --> 00:49:35.550
ALLAN BEAUFOUR: In real time.

00:49:35.550 --> 00:49:37.950
And right now, from a click
hits our website to it's

00:49:37.950 --> 00:49:41.890
available for querying in
BigQuery, it's 45 seconds.

00:49:41.890 --> 00:49:45.119
So that's pretty fast.

00:49:45.119 --> 00:49:46.160
DAMIAN LAWLOR: And Peter?

